{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NMQn7yNmjw61jS5jPUSRmd","type":"tool_result","content":"   100→\n   101→## 4. PR Conventions\n   102→\n   103→### Title format\n   104→\n   105→Include Linear ticket numbers when applicable:\n   106→\n   107→```\n   108→Fixes: Sentinel drains, telemetry fixes, rollback QoL (ENG-194, ENG-193)\n   109→Quality-of-Life Improvements Batch 3 (ENG-158, ENG-180, ENG-179)\n   110→CC not exiting Bug (ENG-186)\n   111→```\n   112→\n   113→Linear is connected to GitHub — including the ticket number in the PR title updates the issue status when the PR merges.\n   114→\n   115→### Body format\n   116→\n   117→PR bodies are detailed. For each change, include:\n   118→\n   119→- **Problem** — what was wrong or what was needed\n   120→- **Fix/Approach** — what was done and why\n   121→- **Files** — which files were touched\n   122→\n   123→Batched PRs (multiple related changes) number each change. See PR #110 for the canonical example (22 numbered sections covering sentinel fixes, telemetry, TUI improvements, docs, and tests).\n   124→\n   125→### Changelog entries\n   126→\n   127→Changelog entries are typically **assembled in the PR body first**, then cleaned up or redacted at release time. The format is:\n   128→\n   129→```markdown\n   130→- **Bold feature/fix name** — Description covering the what and why in one sentence.\n   131→```\n   132→\n   133→Examples from the codebase:\n   134→\n   135→```markdown\n   136→- **Sentinels watching `codon.completed` now reliably fire** — Added a second sentinel queue drain after the `codon.completed` event emission.\n   137→- **Remote hank cache: hash-based freshness** — Branch caches now validate via `git ls-remote` instead of a 1-hour TTL.\n   138→```\n   139→\n   140→### Labels and milestones\n   141→\n   142→Not actively used. Tracking lives in Linear.\n   143→\n   144→### Merge method\n   145→\n   146→**Squash merge** is the standard. This collapses the branch into a single commit on develop with the PR title as the message.\n   147→\n   148→Rebasing is used when working on sequential changes on the same branch. Force-push to a feature branch is okay if you're sure nobody else is on it. Otherwise, cross-merge.\n   149→\n   150→### Attribution and accountability\n   151→\n   152→- **Humans are responsible for all activity.** Nothing is committed or written that hasn't been read end-to-end by a human. Agents operate under their human's supervision and responsibility.\n   153→- **Always quote AI agents.** If a PR body, plan, or document was written by an agent, say so (e.g., \"generated by Opus 4.6, reviewed by @hrishioa\"). If an agent produced a code change, the PR should note which parts were agent-generated. See PR #113 for an example.\n   154→- **Do not add AI tools as committers** in git commits. The human who reviewed and approved the work is the author.\n   155→\n   156→---\n   157→\n   158→## 5. Code Style\n   159→\n   160→- **Language**: TypeScript, ES2022 target, strict mode\n   161→- **Runtime**: Bun (for execution, package management, and testing)\n   162→- **Formatter/Linter**: Biome 2.x — 2-space indent, 100-char line width, LF endings\n   163→  - Covers: `server/`, `tests/e2e/`, `tests/utils/`, `tests/unit/`\n   164→  - Does not cover: `scripts/`, `tests/integration/`, `learning/`\n   165→- **Line endings**: LF everywhere (enforced by `.gitattributes`)\n   166→- **Type checking**: `bun run tc` (runs `tsc --noEmit`)\n   167→\n   168→Run before pushing:\n   169→\n   170→```bash\n   171→bun run lint      # biome check\n   172→bun run tc        # typecheck\n   173→```\n   174→\n   175→---\n   176→\n   177→## 6. Testing\n   178→\n   179→### Test hierarchy (run in this order)\n   180→\n   181→1. **Unit tests** — `bun run test:unit` — fast, no external deps, mock-based\n   182→2. **Integration tests** — `bun run test:integration` — component interactions, mock LLM providers, no API keys needed\n   183→3. **E2E init tests** — `bun run test:e2e:init` — tests the `--init` scaffolding flow (needs real harness binaries)\n   184→4. **Full E2E** — `bun run test` — includes happy-path and rollback tests (needs API keys: Claude, Gemini, Codex)\n   185→\n   186→Tests can be expensive (real LLM calls in E2E). Start with unit, widen as confidence is needed.\n   187→\n   188→### CI matrix\n   189→\n   190→CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on every push to `develop` and PRs targeting it:\n   191→\n   192→- **Lint + typecheck** on ubuntu\n   193→- **Unit + integration** on ubuntu, windows, macos\n   194→- **Init E2E** (normal, npx, binary modes) on ubuntu, windows, macos\n   195→- Uses composite actions in `.github/actions/` for environment setup, test execution, and log dumping on failure\n   196→- E2E tests have retry logic (3 attempts, 10s between retries) because harness processes can be flaky\n   197→\n   198→### Test area\n   199→\n   200→Tests use `tests/test-area/` for temporary files and `tests/test-results/` for output. Both are gitignored.\n   201→\n   202→---\n   203→\n   204→## 7. Release Process\n   205→\n   206→### Scripts\n   207→\n   208→| Script                        | Purpose                                                          |\n   209→| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   210→| `scripts/release.ts`          | Version bump, changelog stamp, merge to release/alpha, tag, push |\n   211→| `scripts/sync-public.ts`      | Snapshot private repo → transform → push to public repo          |\n   212→| `scripts/test-sync-local.ts`  | Local dry-run of the sync process                                |\n   213→| `scripts/build.ts`            | Build the npm package (dist/)                                    |\n   214→| `scripts/build-executable.ts` | Build standalone Bun binaries for 5 platforms                    |\n   215→| `scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | Generate JSON schemas from Zod types                             |\n   216→\n   217→### Release flow (private → npm + binaries)\n   218→\n   219→```bash\n   220→# 1. Dry-run validation (recommended before every release)\n   221→bun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run\n   222→\n   223→# 2. Interactive release\n   224→bun scripts/release.ts minor\n   225→\n   226→# 3. Headless release (for agents)\n   227→bun scripts/release.ts patch --yes\n   228→```\n   229→\n   230→The release script has two phases:\n   231→\n   232→**Phase 1 — Validation** (no changes made):\n   233→\n   234→- Checks branch (must be develop), clean tree, remote sync\n   235→- Computes new version, checks tag availability\n   236→- Validates CHANGELOG has entries in [Unreleased]\n   237→- Tests that develop → release/alpha merge would succeed (no conflicts)\n   238→- Tests schema generation works\n   239→- Runs typecheck and lint (skip with `--skip-checks`)\n   240→- Prints a plan summary\n   241→\n   242→**Phase 2 — Execute** (with automatic rollback on failure):\n   243→\n   244→1. Bump version in package.json\n   245→2. Stamp [Unreleased] → [version] - date in CHANGELOG\n   246→3. Regenerate JSON schemas\n   247→4. Commit \"Release vX.Y.Z\" on develop\n   248→5. Add empty [Unreleased] template, commit\n   249→6. Push develop\n   250→7. Checkout release/alpha, merge develop, strip internal files (intermediates/, external-docs/, CLAUDE.md, CONTRIBUTING.md)\n   251→8. Create tag on release/alpha\n   252→9. Push release/alpha + tag (triggers CI)\n   253→10. Return to develop\n   254→\n   255→If any step fails after changes begin, the script attempts automatic rollback (reset branches to saved checkpoints, delete tags). If changes already reached remote, it prints manual recovery commands.\n   256→\n   257→### What CI does on tag push (private repo)\n   258→\n   259→`.github/workflows/release.yml` triggers on `v*` tags:\n   260→\n   261→1. **publish-npm** — typecheck, lint, tests, then `npm publish` as `@southbridgeai/hankweave` (restricted)\n   262→2. **build-executables** — builds Bun binaries for linux-x64, linux-arm64, darwin-x64, darwin-arm64, windows-x64, with smoke tests on testable platforms\n   263→3. **create-release** — downloads artifacts, creates GitHub release with binaries and changelog-derived release notes\n   264→\n   265→### Sync to public\n   266→\n   267→After the private release CI passes, manually trigger the \"Sync to Public\" workflow on GitHub:\n   268→\n   269→```\n   270→Actions → Sync to Public → Run workflow → version: 0.4.1\n   271→```\n   272→\n   273→Or test locally first:\n   274→\n   275→```bash\n   276→# Dry run (no push)\n   277→bun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version 0.4.1\n   278→\n   279→# Actually push\n   280→bun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version 0.4.1 --push\n   281→```\n   282→\n   283→The sync:\n   284→\n   285→1. Checks out the private repo at the version tag\n   286→2. Clones the public repo's release/alpha branch\n   287→3. Builds a snapshot: rsync (excluding internal files) → overlay public-release-files → remove ci.yml → transform package.json\n   288→4. Extracts changelog (aggregates all versions since last public release)\n   289→5. Replaces the public repo's tree with the snapshot\n   290→6. Commits, tags, pushes to public repo\n   291→\n   292→The public repo's tag push triggers its own `release.yml`: typecheck, lint, build, `npm publish --access public` as `hankweave`, build binaries, create GitHub release.\n   293→\n   294→### Version guidance\n   295→\n   296→- **patch** — bug fixes, docs, minor improvements\n   297→- **minor** — new features, behavioral changes, UX overhauls\n   298→- **major** — breaking changes (hasn't happened yet, pre-1.0)\n   299→\n   300→---\n   301→\n   302→## 8. CHANGELOG Conventions\n   303→\n   304→Format follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/). Structure:\n   305→\n   306→```markdown\n   307→## [Unreleased]\n   308→\n   309→### Added\n   310→\n   311→- **Feature name** — Description.\n   312→\n   313→### Changed\n   314→\n   315→- **Change name** — Description.\n   316→\n   317→### Fixed\n   318→\n   319→- **Fix name** — Description.\n   320→\n   321→## [0.4.1] - 2026-02-13\n   322→\n   323→...\n   324→```\n   325→\n   326→Rules:\n   327→\n   328→- Always have an `[Unreleased]` section at the top\n   329→- Entries use **bold name** — description format\n   330→- Group entries under Added/Changed/Fixed subsections\n   331→- The release script stamps [Unreleased] → [version] - date automatically\n   332→- Entries are assembled during PR work, cleaned up at release time\n   333→- If a version wasn't synced to public, it can be consolidated (e.g., v0.4.0 content was rolled into v0.4.1 because v0.4.0 was internal-only)\n   334→\n   335→---\n   336→\n   337→## 9. Key Files Reference\n   338→\n   339→| File                                   | Purpose                                                                       |\n   340→| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n   341→| `server/index.ts`                      | CLI entry point — arg parsing, routing to commands                            |\n   342→| `server/hankweave-runtime.ts`          | Core runtime — codon execution, state management, event emission              |\n   343→| `server/config.ts`                     | 5-layer config resolution (defaults → hankweave.json → hank.json → env → CLI) |\n   344→| `server/basic-tui.ts`                  | Built-in terminal UI (WebSocket client)                                       |\n   345→| `server/codon-runner.ts`               | Runs individual codons via harness managers                                   |\n   346→| `server/claude-agent-sdk-manager.ts`   | Claude Code SDK integration                                                   |\n   347→| `server/shim-process-manager.ts`       | Process manager for shim-based harnesses (Codex, Gemini)                      |\n   348→| `server/remote-hank.ts`                | Git-based remote hank fetching and caching                                    |\n   349→| `server/sentinels/`                    | Sentinel system (event-triggered observers)                                   |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a77b2755-3bdb-4c5a-a31c-e6ef769bd1ab","uuid":"8137a5df-fdd9-4b49-b147-55338edac774","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md","content":"\n## 4. PR Conventions\n\n### Title format\n\nInclude Linear ticket numbers when applicable:\n\n```\nFixes: Sentinel drains, telemetry fixes, rollback QoL (ENG-194, ENG-193)\nQuality-of-Life Improvements Batch 3 (ENG-158, ENG-180, ENG-179)\nCC not exiting Bug (ENG-186)\n```\n\nLinear is connected to GitHub — including the ticket number in the PR title updates the issue status when the PR merges.\n\n### Body format\n\nPR bodies are detailed. For each change, include:\n\n- **Problem** — what was wrong or what was needed\n- **Fix/Approach** — what was done and why\n- **Files** — which files were touched\n\nBatched PRs (multiple related changes) number each change. See PR #110 for the canonical example (22 numbered sections covering sentinel fixes, telemetry, TUI improvements, docs, and tests).\n\n### Changelog entries\n\nChangelog entries are typically **assembled in the PR body first**, then cleaned up or redacted at release time. The format is:\n\n```markdown\n- **Bold feature/fix name** — Description covering the what and why in one sentence.\n```\n\nExamples from the codebase:\n\n```markdown\n- **Sentinels watching `codon.completed` now reliably fire** — Added a second sentinel queue drain after the `codon.completed` event emission.\n- **Remote hank cache: hash-based freshness** — Branch caches now validate via `git ls-remote` instead of a 1-hour TTL.\n```\n\n### Labels and milestones\n\nNot actively used. Tracking lives in Linear.\n\n### Merge method\n\n**Squash merge** is the standard. This collapses the branch into a single commit on develop with the PR title as the message.\n\nRebasing is used when working on sequential changes on the same branch. Force-push to a feature branch is okay if you're sure nobody else is on it. Otherwise, cross-merge.\n\n### Attribution and accountability\n\n- **Humans are responsible for all activity.** Nothing is committed or written that hasn't been read end-to-end by a human. Agents operate under their human's supervision and responsibility.\n- **Always quote AI agents.** If a PR body, plan, or document was written by an agent, say so (e.g., \"generated by Opus 4.6, reviewed by @hrishioa\"). If an agent produced a code change, the PR should note which parts were agent-generated. See PR #113 for an example.\n- **Do not add AI tools as committers** in git commits. The human who reviewed and approved the work is the author.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Code Style\n\n- **Language**: TypeScript, ES2022 target, strict mode\n- **Runtime**: Bun (for execution, package management, and testing)\n- **Formatter/Linter**: Biome 2.x — 2-space indent, 100-char line width, LF endings\n  - Covers: `server/`, `tests/e2e/`, `tests/utils/`, `tests/unit/`\n  - Does not cover: `scripts/`, `tests/integration/`, `learning/`\n- **Line endings**: LF everywhere (enforced by `.gitattributes`)\n- **Type checking**: `bun run tc` (runs `tsc --noEmit`)\n\nRun before pushing:\n\n```bash\nbun run lint      # biome check\nbun run tc        # typecheck\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Testing\n\n### Test hierarchy (run in this order)\n\n1. **Unit tests** — `bun run test:unit` — fast, no external deps, mock-based\n2. **Integration tests** — `bun run test:integration` — component interactions, mock LLM providers, no API keys needed\n3. **E2E init tests** — `bun run test:e2e:init` — tests the `--init` scaffolding flow (needs real harness binaries)\n4. **Full E2E** — `bun run test` — includes happy-path and rollback tests (needs API keys: Claude, Gemini, Codex)\n\nTests can be expensive (real LLM calls in E2E). Start with unit, widen as confidence is needed.\n\n### CI matrix\n\nCI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on every push to `develop` and PRs targeting it:\n\n- **Lint + typecheck** on ubuntu\n- **Unit + integration** on ubuntu, windows, macos\n- **Init E2E** (normal, npx, binary modes) on ubuntu, windows, macos\n- Uses composite actions in `.github/actions/` for environment setup, test execution, and log dumping on failure\n- E2E tests have retry logic (3 attempts, 10s between retries) because harness processes can be flaky\n\n### Test area\n\nTests use `tests/test-area/` for temporary files and `tests/test-results/` for output. Both are gitignored.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Release Process\n\n### Scripts\n\n| Script                        | Purpose                                                          |\n| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `scripts/release.ts`          | Version bump, changelog stamp, merge to release/alpha, tag, push |\n| `scripts/sync-public.ts`      | Snapshot private repo → transform → push to public repo          |\n| `scripts/test-sync-local.ts`  | Local dry-run of the sync process                                |\n| `scripts/build.ts`            | Build the npm package (dist/)                                    |\n| `scripts/build-executable.ts` | Build standalone Bun binaries for 5 platforms                    |\n| `scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | Generate JSON schemas from Zod types                             |\n\n### Release flow (private → npm + binaries)\n\n```bash\n# 1. Dry-run validation (recommended before every release)\nbun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run\n\n# 2. Interactive release\nbun scripts/release.ts minor\n\n# 3. Headless release (for agents)\nbun scripts/release.ts patch --yes\n```\n\nThe release script has two phases:\n\n**Phase 1 — Validation** (no changes made):\n\n- Checks branch (must be develop), clean tree, remote sync\n- Computes new version, checks tag availability\n- Validates CHANGELOG has entries in [Unreleased]\n- Tests that develop → release/alpha merge would succeed (no conflicts)\n- Tests schema generation works\n- Runs typecheck and lint (skip with `--skip-checks`)\n- Prints a plan summary\n\n**Phase 2 — Execute** (with automatic rollback on failure):\n\n1. Bump version in package.json\n2. Stamp [Unreleased] → [version] - date in CHANGELOG\n3. Regenerate JSON schemas\n4. Commit \"Release vX.Y.Z\" on develop\n5. Add empty [Unreleased] template, commit\n6. Push develop\n7. Checkout release/alpha, merge develop, strip internal files (intermediates/, external-docs/, CLAUDE.md, CONTRIBUTING.md)\n8. Create tag on release/alpha\n9. Push release/alpha + tag (triggers CI)\n10. Return to develop\n\nIf any step fails after changes begin, the script attempts automatic rollback (reset branches to saved checkpoints, delete tags). If changes already reached remote, it prints manual recovery commands.\n\n### What CI does on tag push (private repo)\n\n`.github/workflows/release.yml` triggers on `v*` tags:\n\n1. **publish-npm** — typecheck, lint, tests, then `npm publish` as `@southbridgeai/hankweave` (restricted)\n2. **build-executables** — builds Bun binaries for linux-x64, linux-arm64, darwin-x64, darwin-arm64, windows-x64, with smoke tests on testable platforms\n3. **create-release** — downloads artifacts, creates GitHub release with binaries and changelog-derived release notes\n\n### Sync to public\n\nAfter the private release CI passes, manually trigger the \"Sync to Public\" workflow on GitHub:\n\n```\nActions → Sync to Public → Run workflow → version: 0.4.1\n```\n\nOr test locally first:\n\n```bash\n# Dry run (no push)\nbun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version 0.4.1\n\n# Actually push\nbun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version 0.4.1 --push\n```\n\nThe sync:\n\n1. Checks out the private repo at the version tag\n2. Clones the public repo's release/alpha branch\n3. Builds a snapshot: rsync (excluding internal files) → overlay public-release-files → remove ci.yml → transform package.json\n4. Extracts changelog (aggregates all versions since last public release)\n5. Replaces the public repo's tree with the snapshot\n6. Commits, tags, pushes to public repo\n\nThe public repo's tag push triggers its own `release.yml`: typecheck, lint, build, `npm publish --access public` as `hankweave`, build binaries, create GitHub release.\n\n### Version guidance\n\n- **patch** — bug fixes, docs, minor improvements\n- **minor** — new features, behavioral changes, UX overhauls\n- **major** — breaking changes (hasn't happened yet, pre-1.0)\n\n---\n\n## 8. CHANGELOG Conventions\n\nFormat follows [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/). Structure:\n\n```markdown\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Added\n\n- **Feature name** — Description.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **Change name** — Description.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **Fix name** — Description.\n\n## [0.4.1] - 2026-02-13\n\n...\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- Always have an `[Unreleased]` section at the top\n- Entries use **bold name** — description format\n- Group entries under Added/Changed/Fixed subsections\n- The release script stamps [Unreleased] → [version] - date automatically\n- Entries are assembled during PR work, cleaned up at release time\n- If a version wasn't synced to public, it can be consolidated (e.g., v0.4.0 content was rolled into v0.4.1 because v0.4.0 was internal-only)\n\n---\n\n## 9. Key Files Reference\n\n| File                                   | Purpose                                                                       |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `server/index.ts`                      | CLI entry point — arg parsing, routing to commands                            |\n| `server/hankweave-runtime.ts`          | Core runtime — codon execution, state management, event emission              |\n| `server/config.ts`                     | 5-layer config resolution (defaults → hankweave.json → hank.json → env → CLI) |\n| `server/basic-tui.ts`                  | Built-in terminal UI (WebSocket client)                                       |\n| `server/codon-runner.ts`               | Runs individual codons via harness managers                                   |\n| `server/claude-agent-sdk-manager.ts`   | Claude Code SDK integration                                                   |\n| `server/shim-process-manager.ts`       | Process manager for shim-based harnesses (Codex, Gemini)                      |\n| `server/remote-hank.ts`                | Git-based remote hank fetching and caching                                    |\n| `server/sentinels/`                    | Sentinel system (event-triggered observers)                                   |","numLines":250,"startLine":100,"totalLines":406}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T12:49:24.634Z"}
